Lisp missed its timing. When it was above and beyond everything else and a totally different world, it was way too expensive and way too inefficient.Today, when mainstream languages have stolen much from Lisp, the benefit of using a language where you can generally just add a gem, egg, or crate is much higher than the benefit of the one thing that is still unique to Lisp: macros. Reflection is inelegant as hell when you compare, but it will get you almost all the way macros do.At the same time, typed languages have become much more like dynamic languages. The types have disappeared into var and let statements; they are now inferred rather than repeated. Pattern matching has been added, and more—without compromising the benefits for larger teams and larger code bases.Purists will disagree, but I haven't seen a real world problem solved in Lisp that I couldn't also solve in other languages.And yes, I am interested in being proven wrong. I want to know what kind of problems still exist that are that hardcore. But I suspect a big part of it is that those who code in this still think the "hello world" for Java looks something like this:    public class HelloWorld {
      public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Hello, World!");
      }
    }

when in reality it now looks like this:    void main() {
      println("Hello World");
    }

and in C# it looks like this:    Console.WriteLine("Hello, World!");

And comes with full first class support for Linux and Mac.Lisp is an intellectual tower and has given an incredible amount to the programming community in terms of ideas and a playground for people to try things. I'm not denying any of that — it should be celebrated. But my thesis is this: most of that has been moved, copied, stolen, and adapted to mainstream programming languages.

